.TH CECC-CLIENT "1" "August 2026" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cecc\-client \- test client for the libCEC C API
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cecc\-client
[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIserial\-port\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cecc\-client
connects to a CEC adapter and offers a small interactive command loop. It
is the example client for libCEC's plain
.B C
interface, and unlike
.BR cec\-client (1)
it does not link against the library at all: it loads
.I libcec.so.8
with
.BR dlopen (3)
at start up and resolves the
.B libcec_*
entry points by name. That makes it the tool of choice for checking that
the C ABI and the shared library's SONAME are intact, which is the path
every non\-C++ binding takes; for day to day use of an adapter see
.BR cec\-client (1),
which offers a great deal more.
.PP
If no
.I serial\-port
is given, the first adapter found by autodetection is used. Any argument
that is not recognised as an option is taken to be the port.
.PP
Unless
.B \-t
says otherwise the client registers itself on the bus as a recording
device named
.IR "CEC tester" .
It does not become the active source on its own; use the
.B as
command for that.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-log\-level " \fIlevel\fR"
Set the libCEC log level, as the sum of the levels wanted: 1 error,
2 warning, 4 notice, 8 traffic, 16 debug, 31 all. Values outside 1\-31 are
rejected with a diagnostic and the default is kept.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-type " \fItype\fR"
Register as the given device type:
.B p
playback device,
.B r
recording device,
.B t
tuner,
.B a
audio system,
.B x
TV. The default is
.BR r .
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-osd\-name " \fIname\fR"
Use
.I name
as the OSD name shown to other devices on the bus instead of
.IR "CEC tester" .
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-port " \fInumber\fR"
The HDMI port number the adapter is connected to, 1 to 15. Values outside
that range are clamped.
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-base " \fIaddress\fR"
The logical address of the device the adapter is connected to, as a number.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-rom
Take the configuration from the adapter's EEPROM rather than from the
command line.
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-monitor
Start in monitor\-only mode, watching the bus without claiming a logical
address or replying to anything.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-info
Print the libCEC version and library information, then exit.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-list\-devices
List the CEC adapters found on this system, then exit.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-single\-command
Suppress the progress and banner output, so only the result of the command
is printed. Intended for scripts.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-command " \fIcommand\fR"
Run a single
.I command
from the list below and exit, rather than reading commands from standard
input. Implies
.BR \-s .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary listing the options and commands, then exit.
.SH COMMANDS
Commands are read from standard input, one per line, and may also be given
with
.BR \-c .
Each is matched by prefix, so any longer word starting with one of these is
treated as that command.
.TP
.B as
Broadcast an active source message, making this device the active source.
With
.B \-s
the client then waits up to 15 seconds for the switch to complete.
.TP
.B ea
Ask the audio system to turn system audio mode on.
.TP
.B da
Ask the audio system to turn system audio mode off.
.TP
.B gas
Print the audio system's audio status, in hexadecimal.
.TP
.B gsam
Print whether system audio mode is on.
.TP
.B scan
Scan the bus and print, for every device found, its logical and physical
address, vendor, CEC version, OSD name, power status, and whether it is the
active source.
.TP
.BR q ", " quit
Exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B cecc\-client
exits 0 on success, and 1 if no adapter could be detected or the adapter
could not be opened.
.SH EXAMPLES
Check that the installed shared library can be loaded through the C API and
report its version:
.PP
.RS
.B cecc\-client \-i
.RE
.PP
List the devices on the bus in a script, printing nothing else:
.PP
.RS
.B cecc\-client \-s \-c scan
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cec\-client (1),
.BR pyCecClient (1)
.PP
